//! CapCraft on phones: Concat's engine as a native library for the Android
//! app in `android/`, with Kotlin bindings generated by
//! [UniFFI](https://mozilla.github.io/uniffi-rs).
//!
//! The phone app is one more frontend beside Concat's own window. It shows a
//! timeline laid out the way phone editors lay theirs out ([`lanes`]): one
//! gapless main track of video and stills, overlay rows above it, rows of
//! text and stickers, rows of effects and filters, and audio rows. Each row
//! is a lane of the engine's timeline, and every edit is one engine command
//! or one batch of them, so each is one undo step.
//!
//! Time crosses the bridge as whole microseconds, never as floating-point
//! seconds; the engine's document keeps seconds, and the conversion happens
//! here, at the boundary.
//!
//! Threading: every method may be called from any thread. The session's lock
//! is held only while a call reads or edits the document; pictures, sound and
//! exports are made outside it. The app calls in from its own threads, never
//! from the main one.
//!
//! No unsafe code, except the two JNI entry points in `android` that hand
//! the Java VM and the app's context to the sound device.
#![deny(unsafe_code)]

